unit PascalCommon.TlsTransportTests;

{$mode delphi}{$H+}

{ GENERATED FILE — produced by tools/gen_fpc_mirror.py from
  tests/Unit/PascalCommon.TlsTransportTests.pas (DUnitX). Do not edit by hand: edit the DUnitX
  master and run the script again. }

{ Loopback tests for PcTlsConnect, PcTlsListen and PcTlsWrap, on both TLS
  backends, with the certificates of tests/pki.

  Ported from pascal-pipes-faa's Pipes.TlsTests, rewritten: those tests go
  through pipes' TPipeServer/TPipeClient, these through the endpoints. The
  cases kept: the round trip, the client validating the server by IP and by
  name, mTLS accepted with the client's identity, and the refusals (no
  certificate, another CA, self-signed, the twin CA), the server's handshake
  deadline. Half of them are refusals, and that half is what proves there is
  authentication: a suite that only checked "the right client connects"
  would pass with authentication broken.

  New here: the client validating the server against a private CA on
  SChannel (pipes' SChannel client ignored CaFile), a wrong name and an
  unknown CA refused, the client's handshake deadline, a full-duplex bulk
  transfer over TLS (many records, a reader and a writer on each endpoint:
  the SChannel concurrency question of docs/transport-design.md), CloseAbort
  and SetIoDeadline through TLS, and Read before Handshake.

  One fixture, parameterized by the client's and the server's backend. The
  base runs tbDefault on both sides (SChannel on Windows, OpenSSL elsewhere).
  On Windows three subclasses run the same tests as OpenSSL-OpenSSL and
  crossed (an OpenSSL client against an SChannel server, and the reverse),
  in the same run (T3). When OpenSSL can't be loaded on Windows (no 32-bit
  DLL on the PATH, for a Win32 build), those three print why and pass; off
  Windows OpenSSL is the only backend and its absence fails every test.

  DUnitX master: the FPCUnit mirror in tests/Unit/fpc is generated by
  tools/gen_fpc_mirror.py. }

